Canonicalization

The purpose of these conventions is to minimize the ongoing effort required to maintain and restore a system while preserving its specified behavior and explicitly declared state.

A canonicalized system is declaratively defined with NixOS, uses an ephemeral root filesystem with explicitly preserved state, and has a home directory managed as a Git repository with a whitelist `.gitignore`. The home repository organizes projects in a Go-style path layout (for example, `github.com/owner/project`). Each project is a Git submodule and a Nix flake with its own whitelist `.gitignore`. Flake repositories follow a purpose-built subset of the https://github.com/numtide/blueprint directory layout: a README explains the repository's intent, packages contain a single source file, tests describe expected behavior, and code implements it.

`git canonicalization` helps in creating and maintaining these home and flake repository conventions.

Initialize the home repository:

```sh
cd
git init
printf '*\n!/.gitignore\n!/.gitmodules\n' > .gitignore # add other required paths, such as .ssh, to the whitelist
git add .gitignore
git commit -m 'Initialize home repository'
```

Add and initialize a project submodule:

```sh
# Create the empty remote before starting this workflow.
git submodule add --force git@github.com:owner/demo.git ~/github.com/owner/demo
cd ~/github.com/owner/demo
git canonicalization init . # initializes a flake repository with these conventions
git canonicalization add <type> <name> # scaffolds a package in the current flake repository
nix fmt # runs conventional formatters and strict linters
nix flake check # verifies the canonical structure and runs builds and tests
```
